,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,4340,1657,1931,5109,525,537,304,279,7947,315,279,729,26107,69,2075,8,284,1124,37018,90,16,15170,87,12,21,19,92,488,1124,37018,90,16,15170,87,61,17,12,21,19,92,488,1124,37018,90,16,15170,87,61,18,12,21,19,92,93,30,14085,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nHow many real numbers are not in the domain of the function $$f(x) = \\frac{1}{x-64} + \\frac{1}{x^2-64} + \\frac{1}{x^3-64}~?$$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"b68a5146a2d9cf2aa09c80937420039abe50d554fdb64aedef76f2899dcfd827"},"seconds":10.72136926651001,"seed":484051615070236425,"stop":"eos","text":"To determine how many real numbers are not in the domain of the function \\( f(x) = \\frac{1}{x-64} + \\frac{1}{x^2-64} + \\frac{1}{x^3-64} \\), we need to identify the values of \\( x \\) that make any of the denominators zero. The function is undefined at these points.\n\nFirst, consider the denominator \\( x - 64 \\). This is zero when:\n\\[ x - 64 = 0 \\implies x = 64. \\]\n\nNext, consider the denominator \\( x^2 - 64 \\). This can be factored as:\n\\[ x^2 - 64 = (x - 8)(x + 8). \\]\nSo, this is zero when:\n\\[ x - 8 = 0 \\implies x = 8, \\]\nor\n\\[ x + 8 = 0 \\implies x = -8. \\]\n\nFinally, consider the denominator \\( x^3 - 64 \\). This can be factored using the difference of cubes:\n\\[ x^3 - 64 = (x - 4)(x^2 + 4x + 16). \\]\nSo, this is zero when:\n\\[ x - 4 = 0 \\implies x = 4. \\]\nThe quadratic \\( x^2 + 4x + 16 \\) has no real roots because its discriminant is negative:\n\\[ 4^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 16 = 16 - 64 = -48. \\]\n\nThus, the values of \\( x \\) that are not in the domain of the function are \\( x = 64 \\), \\( x = 8 \\), \\( x = -8 \\), and \\( x = 4 \\). These are four distinct real numbers.\n\nTherefore, the number of real numbers not in the domain of the function is:\n\\[ \\boxed{4}. \\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{4}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:974"}
